course: dictionary
lesson: 8020
title: Reflect: send back, show, or think
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word reflect has more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. When something reflects light, sound or heat, it sends it back. «The Moon is not a source: it only reflects sunlight.» The Moon does not make its own light, so reflects must mean sends back the light from the Sun.
explain: A mirror or still water also reflects you, which means it shows your image. In «Caimans' eyes glow red-orange in torchlight because a shiny layer behind the eye reflects light», the eye sends the torch light back, so the glow can be seen.
explain: To reflect on something means to think about it carefully. Your teacher might say, «Reflect on your learning», which means think about what went well. The words on and learning are your clues that this meaning is about thinking, not light.
explain: Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. If send back or show does not fit, try think about carefully. The sentence should still make sense.
fact: Reflect means to send back light, sound or heat, or to show an image, like your face in a mirror.
fact: To reflect on something means to think about it carefully.
fact: «Reflect» can also mean to show something, like a face that reflects how someone feels.
fact: «Water, sand and snow reflect UV», so you can get sunburnt even under an umbrella.
fact: «Planets and the Moon do not make their own light; they reflect sunlight.»
exercise: «The Moon is not a source: it only reflects sunlight.» What does reflects mean here? | *Sends back light from the Sun | Shines its own light | Goes dark | Turns into the Sun
exercise: Your teacher writes, «Reflect on your learning.» What does she want you to do? | Send your work back to the printer | Shine a torch at the board | Look in a mirror | *Think carefully about what went well
exercise: Why do light clothes keep you cooler? Think about reflect. | Because they are heavier | Because they hold more sunlight | Because they make you sweat more | *Because white and shiny things reflect heat
test: «Water, sand and snow reflect UV, so you can burn even under an umbrella.» What does reflect mean here? | Freeze solid | *Send back | Turn into rain | Soak up and keep
test: Which word means think about something carefully? Here is a clue: «Please reflect on how you could improve your story.» | Send back | Shine | *Think | Show
exam: Which meaning fits best in this sentence? «Mia's sad face reflected how she felt about losing.» | Sent back, like light | Thought about it very carefully, and then forgot it | Shone a torch | *Showed, like a picture of her feelings
